  {
    "episode_id": 89151,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89151.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Trey, I appreciate it, honor to be on. You know, I grew up in Bucks County, Pennsylvania. I used to collect baseball cards. I used to go to baseball cards shows. We had a couple of us, my buddies in sixth and seventh grade. We used to set up baseball cards shows, sell baseball cards, trade baseball cards. Played little league wasn't very good at it. It was better at the field than hitting my memories of when I was up at the plate, people saying, watch the bun, because they used to have me bun often enough, because I couldn't hit. So everyone used to chant that. But we had a pretty good team, at least when I was 12, the expos. One of my favorite things being at Congress is that someone had tweeted out the roster of the expos for my little league team. And I got to catch up with some of the folks who were there. And, you know, played street hockey, played touch football, went to Rolling Hills High and in junior high school and probably around 9th or 10th grade I started actually developing some interest in politics at current affairs not in elective part but I used to write letters to the editor to the Bucks County Courier Times and I often tell the story my first sort of thing that was published at 14 was about the first Gulf War And the columnist of the Bucks County Courier Times said, read this 14-year-old's Lip Couture. It was the first President Bush. And I was so excited. I thought the president was going to read my op-ed. Now I realize I can write in the New York Times, the president doesn't read your op-ed.",
