        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker recently wrote a book on decision-making.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Running against an incumbent carries substantial reputational and professional risk.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Decision-making under high personal risk is a meaningful topic worth writing about.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Personal experience provides credible insight into the emotional toll of political challenges.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "People close to the challenger likely advised against running due to career concerns.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "There exists a widely shared perception that incumbents are nearly unbeatable.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 89151,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89151.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "So I did it twice. First and after law school, I came in and challenged and incumbent in my own party where, you know, as you know, if you step into, you go into a room and your own party folks will be going in the other direction if you're challenging and incumbent. But at the time, this incumbent had voted for the war in Iraq. I was opposed to the war. And I wanted to get that perspective out and challenge him. And people said, you've got a future. Why are you doing this? You're going to end your career before it even began. The irony of all of that is that, you know, I'm not saying it works out like this for everyone, but I got killed in that first campaign. I mean, Latinos got 70 some percent. I got 20 percent. And he actually ended up becoming a mentor of sorts. He sort of admired the Hutspa. And he said, look, if you're not a movie star, you don't just get up and run for the United States Congress. This is a more organic process. And it actually He helped open some doors in the Democratic Party and was pretty gracious about the whole thing. I'm not recommending it for other folks necessarily, the sort of city challenge will end up becoming someone who becomes a mentor, but in my case, in this situation, it worked out.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I ran against an incumbent member of my own political party.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The incumbent supported the Iraq War while I opposed it.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "I lost the first congressional campaign I ran in by a significant margin.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Latinos made up approximately 70% of the voters in that district.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "I received around 20% of the vote in that election.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The defeated incumbent later became a mentor to me.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The former incumbent advised that running for U.S. Congress requires an organic process rather than sudden fame.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The former incumbent helped me gain access to opportunities within the Democratic Party.",
