    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "I did think about it for the reasons you said. I mean, these open Senate seats don't come up that often. And I had a path not saying I would have won, but certainly would have been a contender. I decided to guess it for a couple of reasons. I actually think the House of Representatives has become one of the most exciting places to serve. Think about on both sides, on the left and the right. Some of the most famous people and the most influential people are these days in the House of Representatives. The calculus has changed. And I shouldn't take a shot at all, but I once choked around saying, you know, that was at Christie on this week, every other week. And I said, just pick a random person from the Republican House caucus, and you get more of a sense of where the country is, or pick a random person for the Democratic caucus. These are folks who are much more in touch with the grassroots, and it's a vibrant place to serve. So that was one of it. Two, I represent Silicon Valley. with AI and technology. That's an enormous platform. Three, you know, I kind of think of myself as this hybrid from Pennsylvania and Silicon Valley. If there's any grounding in my political career, it's remembering the kids I grew up with when we root for the Eagles and the Phillies. And I think being from the Valley actually allows me to go to rural towns, factory towns, talking about bringing new jobs, manufacturing in a way. that may not have the same ability to do that representing the state. So those were some of the factors, but, you know, if I'd run it, it would have been a probably a one out of three shot because there's no guarantee in life. And those are pretty decent odds for a sedacy.",
