  {
    "episode_id": 89151,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89151.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "I think it was going to say exactly that. It's the faith that people have in the power of government to do good. And this is why I go on. You were trying to say, go on different shows and talk to different people. It's because I genuinely don't think anyone has a monopoly on the truth. I mean, I have a stronger belief in the role of government to help us bring manufacturing to help create a quality of economic opportunity. But you know, if we did have pushback, if people were to say, hey, bro, that government isn't working that well here. These programs aren't working that well here. have you thought about the negative impact of the body you're calling for spending, then there wouldn't be the same kind of rigor and scrutiny. I think one of the best parts of the American system is it forces you to actually test your ideas and have pushback. Now some of that when it turns into ad hominem attacks and other things is obviously not the best of our tradition. But for me, I guess I have enough humility that the 250 years of American history has a collective wisdom to it that doesn't mean you don't push for new ideas, but that you've got to test it within that tradition. And I really think the basic difference comes from how much people think government can work towards ends that are often common ends.",
