  {
    "episode_id": 89151,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89151.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "That's a deep question. You know, when I used to teach, there were two things that I would assign students that get to some of these questions. One was Abraham Lincoln's Lyceum Address. And he gave that when he was in his late 30s. And Lincoln basically said, you know, what happens if in America someone comes up with the ambition of a Napoleon or our founders, and it's not good enough to just be in Congress or governors. They want to start something new and that where the history is full of people with this kind of ambition. And Lincoln says the way to check that ambition is to have a fidelity to the Constitution and to the rule of law and to channel that kind of ambition into a constitutional patriotism. And on the other hand, it came Martin Luther King with his letter from a Birmingham jail where he talks about the moral law and a moral law that supersedes the laws of a nation and when it makes sense to follow your own conscience. And my own grandfather, of course, went to jail in India resisting a British colonialism I guess my reconciliation of that is for most of us, most of the time, follow the law. I mean, this is not one of the things that you just get to decide, I don't like the law. And there are a lot of ways that you can challenge the law through the political process in a country that I still think is the most open to the political system. But for those who really, their conscience cannot abide by them, following the law. What I would say is then do it in the way that King did it peacefully, non-violently, and be willing to have the consequences of that. I mean, King went to jail, and he understood that. And he was willing to go to jail because he understood we were a democracy under laws, but it's his conscience that didn't allow him to do that. So that's how I would try to reconcile the two.",
