  {
    "episode_id": 89151,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89151.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "You mentioned free speech. You're the perfect person. I hear that phrase all the time. As if you and I have a First Amendment right to appear on whatever show we want to appear on tonight, we do not. And you don't have a First Amendment right to address the floor of the house. I don't think you got to be recognized. We hear this, you know, Twitter or fill in the blank is violating my right to free speech. And the way I look at it is it's a private company. And if they have rules and you're not following the rules, so how do you reconcile this belief in free speech with the reality that the First Amendment applies to government, not to private companies?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The First Amendment protects against government censorship, not private entity censorship.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The First Amendment does not grant individuals the right to appear on any television show.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Private companies like Twitter are not bound by the First Amendment.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Individuals do not have a First Amendment right to speak on the floor of the House without being recognized.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "If someone violates a private company’s rules, they may lose access to that platform.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes the First Amendment should be interpreted strictly according to its legal scope.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "People who claim their free speech rights are violated by private platforms likely misunderstand the reach of the First Amendment.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The legitimacy of free speech discourse depends on correctly identifying who enforces restrictions.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Private companies have legitimate authority to set terms of service without infringing on constitutional rights.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Audience members understand the distinction between public and private actors in constitutional law.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Correction / Challenge"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 89151,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89151.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 45,
    "turn_text": "Well, you're absolutely right. In terms of the First Amendment doctrine, I think people throw that around probably loosely. And yeah, Twitter or Facebook can come up with their own rules. A university could come up with its own rules. But I think When people often invoke free speech, they invoke just a sense of what they hope is fairness and not people coming at things with ideological bias. So if you were in your classroom, I imagine have a free speech right to just tell people that, you know, progressives like Connor are totally idiots and don't have any reason to understand what they believe. But that probably wouldn't make you a great educator. And the same way Twitter could have a conversation where they censor left or right perspectives. And that's in their first amendment right. But I think what people are reacting to is a sense of this country that we've lost respect for each other, that we come into things with moral judgment and ideological bias. And they just want folks to respect their point of view and listen to robust debate. And that, to me, is an important value in this country. And I think that value is worth celebrating more, that we should try to persuade people, listen to folks, and not come with the kind of moral judgment we do, assuming that somehow we're better or smarter, but really try to engage in a conversation.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The First Amendment protects individuals from government censorship.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Social media platforms like Twitter and Facebook can establish their own content moderation policies.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Universities can create their own speech-related rules and regulations.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "People who cite free speech often mean fairness and lack of ideological bias rather than legal rights.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a widespread perception that mutual respect in public discourse has declined in the U.S.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "A professor has the legal right to express offensive views in a classroom under free speech protections.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Expressing extreme partisan views in a classroom may undermine effective teaching.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Twitter can choose to censor either left-wing or right-wing perspectives without violating the First Amendment.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "Many people desire respectful listening and robust debate over moral judgment in political discussions.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Promoting persuasion through dialogue, rather than moral superiority, is a valuable civic ideal.",
        "confidence": 0.84
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Legal free speech rights are distinct from ethical or pedagogical responsibilities in educational settings.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Audiences expect institutions like universities and social media companies to uphold norms beyond mere legality.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Moral judgment based on ideology undermines productive democratic discourse.",
